{"name":"Village Square","hex":"#7b6f61","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"village","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A rural habitation of size between a hamlet and a town.","A rural habitation that has a church, but no market."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/village"},{"term":"square","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A polygon with four straight sides of equal length and four right angles; an equilateral rectangle; a regular quadrilateral.","Something characterized by a square, or nearly square, form.\nA cell in a grid."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Shaped like a square (the polygon).","Forming a right angle (90°)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/square"}],"entries":[{"title":"Village","blocks":[{"text":"A village is a human settlement or a residential community, larger than a hamlet but smaller than a town; some geographers describe villages as having between 500 and 2,500 inhabitants. Although villages are often located in rural areas, the term urban village is also applied to certain urban neighborhoods. Villages are normally permanent, with fixed dwellings, however, transient villages also exist. Further, the dwellings of a village are fairly close to one another, not scattered broadly over the landscape, as a dispersed settlement."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Bourton-on-the-Water_2010_PD_09.JPG/330px-Bourton-on-the-Water_2010_PD_09.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Village"},{"title":"Square","blocks":[{"text":"In geometry, a square is a regular quadrilateral. It has four straight sides of equal length and four equal angles. Squares are special cases of rectangles, which have four equal angles, and of rhombuses, which have four equal sides. As with all rectangles, a square's angles are right angles, making adjacent sides perpendicular. The area of a square is the side length multiplied by itself, and so in algebra, multiplying a number by itself is called squaring."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Square_with_offset_tick_marks.svg/330px-Square_with_offset_tick_marks.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Square"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg/500px-Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg/1280px-Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg/500px-Veduta_di_Pacentro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pacentro, Italy","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVeduta_di_Pacentro.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Stroczycy_Bielarus_2008MV04.jpg/500px-Stroczycy_Bielarus_2008MV04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Stroczycy_Bielarus_2008MV04.jpg/500px-Stroczycy_Bielarus_2008MV04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Strochitsy, Belarus, pictured 2008","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStroczycy_Bielarus_2008MV04.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Halkia%2C_Pornainen_2.jpg/500px-Halkia%2C_Pornainen_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Halkia%2C_Pornainen_2.jpg/1280px-Halkia%2C_Pornainen_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wi